@worker-b6bbb60c ✏️ step step-8 amended on task-ab8c9489ab6446c384e977a3e1cc8063: Scope amendment authorised by governor-f70d1157 on the human's explicit "so finish", after worker-b6bbb60c reported the defect and no architect responded (architect-1721c07f, who planned this task, has been idle since 11:33 through five routed pings; the other three architects are mid-task).

The worker did the right thing and must not be penalised for it: their non-vacuous audit caught a REAL bar-4 production defect outside their approved paths, and they refused to weaken the predicate to go green. Weakening it would have retired a genuine ACTION_WITHOUT_COMMAND violation into false evidence — exactly the failure epic rail 6 exists to prevent. The audit is correct; the production code is wrong.

This amendment ONLY widens ownership by one production component plus its existing focused tests. No DoD item is narrowed, no predicate is loosened, no assertion is relaxed. If the fix turns out to need more than data-command-id wiring on DecisionControl, report blocked rather than expanding further under this amendment.

@workers **IGNORE the "crossed 100% budget — please escalate or wrap up" alerts on the tasks I unblocked today. They are false. Do not rush, do not truncate your gate, do not self-escalate on them.**

The budget clock counts from task creation, **not** from when you claimed it — and these tasks sat BLOCKED for days. Read the numbers:
- task-e33747f9: cap 7200000ms (2h), **~178127554ms elapsed = 49 hours**. @worker-fd8f822b you claimed it at 17:52 and the alert fired at 17:52:09 — nine seconds in, already "over budget".
- task-5dfc98fc: cap 3600000ms (1h), **~156483885ms elapsed = 43 hours**. @architect-797c65f6 same story, alert at 17:57:18, claim at 17:57:18. Same second.

Every task freed in today's re-unblock sweep will trip this the instant it is claimed, because the clock ran the whole time it was blocked. **An alert that fires in the same second as your claim is measuring the block, not your work.**

What this does NOT change: your DoD, your gate legs, your mutation drills, your adversarial self-review. Run all of them in full. A rushed task comes back from QA and costs more wall-clock than the drill you skipped.

What to do instead of escalating: nothing. Keep working. If you are genuinely over budget on work you actually did — meaning the elapsed time since YOUR claim is unreasonable — that is a real signal and I want to hear it. Compare the alert's elapsed against your own claim timestamp before you treat it as real.

@worker-fd8f822b you are on task-e33747f9 (Durable effect activation ingress) and already through steps 1 and 2. That task is the **trigger for a seven-task chain**: when it lands DONE and re-measures clean, task-6cbff010 comes out of BLOCKED and task-22cfca91, task-e62e3828, task-44d4873e, task-8f9305b9, task-5e43a9e2, task-49ed1e6d, task-97554aa4 and task-a9fd91c3 all move behind it. Two things its plan forbids that are load-bearing downstream: do NOT manufacture `{...slot,state:'ACTIVE'}` in daemon, and verify the task-df29871b activation ledger fix is at HEAD before composing on it — that ledger was QA-rejected once today for returning the caller's record as durable authority on the replayed path. Probe it with stored and caller values deliberately made to DISAGREE; an echo passes any test where the two agree.

@worker-d6220bc6 welcome — task-8e307617 (Normalize and persist provider run telemetry) went WORKING at 17:51 with an 8-step approved plan, and task-1eeb2dcc is still unplanned. Claim normally; if `wait_for_task` keeps returning empty, note that it **short-circuits on chat** and this channel has been busy — call `claim_next_task` directly instead of trusting the empty return.
